2025-05-01 00:46:28 - Thread-78 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant columns and their potential usage

PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS  
• "publication_number": unique ID of each patent publication – used to join citing vs. cited patents.  
• "assignee": VARIANT array that stores the (raw) assignee names – used to identify patents assigned to “DENSO CORP” and to obtain the citing assignee name.  
• "assignee_harmonized": VARIANT array with cleaned assignee names – alternative source for assignee names.  
• "citation": VARIANT array that stores backward‑citation data (the patents a given publication cites) – used to link a citing patent to the publication numbers of the cited patents.  
• "cpc": VARIANT array that stores CPC codes; each object has a field "code" and (often) "first" = TRUE for the main CPC – used to extract the primary CPC subclass of the citing patent.  
• "filing_date": NUMERIC; filter out patents with NULL or 0 to keep only valid filing dates.  
• "family_id": optional join key if one wants to aggregate across simple families.

PATENTS.PATENTS.CPC_DEFINITION  
• "symbol": full CPC symbol; will be matched (e.g., on the first 4‑char subclass or the complete code) to obtain the textual title.  
• "titleFull": full title of that CPC symbol – needed in the result.  

Typical conditions  
• valid filing dates: t."filing_date" IS NOT NULL AND t."filing_date" > 0  
• DENSO patents (cited side): assignee name ILIKE '%DENSO%'  
• exclude DENSO as citing assignee: citing_name NOT ILIKE '%DENSO%'  
• primary CPC only: cpc_item.value:"first"::BOOLEAN = TRUE  

-----------------------------------------------------------------
Ten exploratory Snowflake SQL queries
-----------------------------------------------------------------

```sql
--Description: 1) Quick look at a few publications whose raw assignee text contains 'DENSO'.
SELECT 
    t."publication_number", 
    a.value:"name"::STRING AS "assignee_name"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS t,
     LATERAL FLATTEN(input => t."assignee") a
WHERE a.value:"name"::STRING ILIKE '%DENSO%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 2) Inspect the structure of the "citation" field for one random DENSO patent.
SELECT 
    t."publication_number",
    f.value AS "citation_object"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS t,
     LATERAL FLATTEN(input => t."citation") f
WHERE t."publication_number" = (
        SELECT MIN("publication_number") 
        FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS,
             LATERAL FLATTEN(input => "assignee") a
        WHERE a.value:"name"::STRING ILIKE '%DENSO%'
      )
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 3) List a sample of cited publication numbers that appear inside the "citation" array.
SELECT 
    DISTINCT f.value:"publication_number"::STRING AS "cited_pub"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS t,
     LATERAL FLATTEN(input => t."citation") f
WHERE f.value:"publication_number" IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 4) Show the structure of the CPC variant and detect the 'first' flag.
SELECT 
    t."publication_number",
    c.value AS "cpc_item"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS t,
     LATERAL FLATTEN(input => t."cpc") c
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 5) Extract the main CPC code (where “first” = TRUE) for a few patents.
SELECT 
    t."publication_number",
    c.value:"code"::STRING      AS "main_cpc_code",
    c.value:"first"::BOOLEAN    AS "is_first_flag"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS t,
     LATERAL FLATTEN(input => t."cpc") c
WHERE c.value:"first"::BOOLEAN = TRUE
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 6) Join a main CPC code to its full title from CPC_DEFINITION.
SELECT 
    c_main."main_cpc_code",
    d."titleFull"
FROM (
    SELECT DISTINCT
           c.value:"code"::STRING AS "main_cpc_code"
    FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p,
         LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."cpc") c
    WHERE c.value:"first"::BOOLEAN = TRUE
    LIMIT 100
) c_main
LEFT JOIN PATENTS.PATENTS.CPC_DEFINITION d
       ON d."symbol" = c_main."main_cpc_code"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 7) Identify publication numbers of patents assigned to DENSO (potentially the cited side).
SELECT 
    DISTINCT p."publication_number"
FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p,
     LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."assignee") a
WHERE a.value:"name"::STRING ILIKE '%DENSO%'
  AND p."filing_date" IS NOT NULL
  AND p."filing_date" > 0
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 8) Retrieve citing patents (with valid filing dates) that reference any DENSO patent via citations.
WITH denso_pubs AS (
    SELECT DISTINCT p."publication_number"
    FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p,
         LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."assignee") a
    WHERE a.value:"name"::STRING ILIKE '%DENSO%'
),
citing AS (
    SELECT 
        t."publication_number"        AS "citing_pub",
        f.value:"publication_number"::STRING AS "cited_pub"
    FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS t,
         LATERAL FLATTEN(input => t."citation") f
    WHERE t."filing_date" IS NOT NULL
      AND t."filing_date" > 0
)
SELECT *
FROM citing
WHERE cited_pub IN (SELECT * FROM denso_pubs)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 9) Add citing assignee names (excluding any that contain 'DENSO').
WITH citing_set AS (
    -- reuse query 8
    SELECT DISTINCT t."publication_number"
    FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS t,
         LATERAL FLATTEN(input => t."citation") f,
         LATERAL FLATTEN(input => t."assignee") a,
         (
              SELECT DISTINCT p."publication_number" AS "denso_pub"
              FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p,
                   LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."assignee") a2
              WHERE a2.value:"name"::STRING ILIKE '%DENSO%'
         ) d
    WHERE f.value:"publication_number"::STRING = d."denso_pub"
      AND t."filing_date" IS NOT NULL
      AND t."filing_date" > 0
      AND a.value:"name"::STRING NOT ILIKE '%DENSO%'
)
SELECT 
    cs."publication_number" AS "citing_pub"
FROM citing_set cs
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--Description: 10) For each (non‑DENSO) citing assignee, grab the primary CPC subclass and count the citations to DENSO patents; join the CPC title.
WITH denso_pubs AS (
    SELECT DISTINCT p."publication_number"
    FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS p,
         LATERAL FLATTEN(input => p."assignee") a
    WHERE a.value:"name"::STRING ILIKE '%DENSO%'
),
citing AS (
    SELECT 
        t."publication_number"                         AS "citing_pub",
        a.value:"name"::STRING                         AS "citing_assignee",
        c.value:"code"::STRING                         AS "main_cpc_code"
    FROM PATENTS.PATENTS.PUBLICATIONS t
         ,LATERAL FLATTEN(input => t."citation") f
         ,LATERAL FLATTEN(input => t."assignee") a
         ,LATERAL FLATTEN(input => t."cpc")     c
    WHERE f.value:"publication_number"::STRING IN (SELECT * FROM denso_pubs)
      AND t."filing_date" IS NOT NULL
      AND t."filing_date" > 0
      AND a.value:"name"::STRING NOT ILIKE '%DENSO%'
      AND c.value:"first"::BOOLEAN = TRUE
)
SELECT 
    citing_assignee,
    d."titleFull"                                      AS "cpc_title",
    COUNT(*)                                           AS "num_citations"
FROM citing
LEFT JOIN PATENTS.PATENTS.CPC_DEFINITION d
       ON d."symbol" = citing."main_cpc_code"
GROUP BY citing_assignee, d."titleFull"
ORDER BY num_citations DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

These queries progress from inspecting raw data structures to a consolidated result that aligns with the stated analytical task, while always enclosing column names in double quotation marks and limiting outputs to 20 rows for safety.
